VulkanLumen — Cornell prototype with stabilized directional GI

Architecture
  1. Raster HDR + depth + face ID
  2. 16 planar material cards (RGBA8, 64x64; separate lit cards 128x128)
  3. Direct/total Surface Cache radiance atlases (RGBA16F)
  4. 12x8x12 directional world radiance cache
  5. 8x8 directional radiance per 8x8 screen probe
  6. Geometry-aware 3x3 screen-probe-space filter
  7. Depth/face-validated full-resolution indirect composition

This is a Cornell-specific prototype inspired by Epic's Lumen documentation.
It is NOT the complete Unreal Engine Lumen Scene, Screen Probe Gather, or
reflections implementation. The Cornell BVH has 32 triangles including the
ceiling light; external meshes/lights/G-buffers remain unsupported.

Visual-quality correction following the first lit-card/directional-probe test:
  - Secondary rays used nearest-neighbor Surface Cache radiance texels,
    yielding discontinuities on large planar faces. Now manually bilinear
    within each face card to avoid bleeding across adjacent atlas cards.
  - Screen-probe fallback used the single nearest world-space irradiance
    probe, producing abrupt lighting changes at grid cell boundaries.
    Now trilinearly interpolate the SH coefficients of eight valid probes
    ONCE per screen tile, then reuse across all 64 angular bins.
  - Each angular bin formerly retraced the exact same direction, making
    "accumulation" incapable of improving angular aliasing. Directional
    ray samples now jitter *within* their octahedral bin every update;
    history averages genuinely distinct directions in probe space.
  - A one-sample ray result formerly overrode the world radiance estimate
    immediately, creating bright/dark 16x16 blocks. Screen directional
    samples now have a finite sample count and confidence weighting against
    the world-cache prior. The final indirect correction grows with
    directional coverage instead of instantly flashing on first contact.
  - A small 3x3 spatial filter operates on SCREEN PROBES, not on the
    final HDR image. It only mixes the same face with nearby world-space
    hit positions, maintaining material/geometry edges.
  - Fixed compute/fragment barriers: directional gather and metadata must
    be available to the downstream probe-filter compute pass before the
    result is read by the fullscreen composite.

Runtime
  - Material cards update when dirty; one 128x128 direct/total lighting card
    updated per frame to amortize area-light shadow rays.
  - World radiance cache updates 64 probes x 8 rays/frame by default.
  - Screen probes trace 4 of 64 directional bins/frame by default.
  - World cache and screen-probe temporal convergence takes several seconds.
  - There is no full-screen denoiser, TAA, or neural reconstruction.
  - The screen filter is included in the existing GPU 'screen' timing.
  - Default internal raster remains capped at 1280 pixels wide on Retina.

Build / test from vulkan-experiment:
  git pull --ff-only
  rm -f c.lock
  c build run vulkan-experiment

Look for the new shader:
  build/shaders/lumen_screen_filter.comp

The module startup banner now reports "geometry-aware probe filter=3x3".
Compare the previous red/green blockiness and striping on the Cornell walls.
Send a screenshot and GPU cards/GI/scene/screen/composite/total times.
Native MoltenVK shader compilation and M1 visual/performance tests have not
been performed in this environment.

Known issues still to address as separate evidence-based milestones:
  - General mesh-card capture/lighting, not only Cornell planar UVs
  - Robust directional visibility and placement of world probes
  - Screen-space motion-vector reprojection and adaptive probe sampling
  - Dynamic BLAS/TLAS/lighting invalidation, full multibounce scheduling
  - Physically-based materials, rough/smooth specular reflections
  - Public standalone GI/reflection outputs instead of Cornell-only compose

The known working older renderer is preserved in
xt9y/vulkan-systems branch lumen-stage7-bak.

2026-11-08 follow-up: final gather composition and probe visibility
  - Raster HDR now stores emissive and direct Surface Cache lighting only.
    The screen-probe pass adds the sole indirect diffuse term, avoiding
    signed corrections that could create black halos near the emitter.
  - Each screen probe gathers positive incoming radiance with world-SH
    fallback while its 8x8 directional history accumulates.
  - World probes used by screen gathering are normal-facing and BVH-visible
    from the receiving surface, reducing bleeding through boxes.
  - World radiance updates now use deterministic 32-visit interleaved
    directions rather than independent random angles, reducing variance.
  - Expect somewhat higher Screen Probe GPU time because of occlusion rays,
    partly offset by removing world-irradiance sampling from raster fragments.
  - Actual MoltenVK visual and GPU-time validation remains required.

Direct/indirect decomposition:
  Analytic area-light visibility and energy are stored in direct cards.
  World radiance and Screen Probe Gather rays ignore direct hits of the
  emissive panel; they collect radiance from non-emissive lit surfaces.
  This prevents the area-light contribution from being counted twice.

2026-11-08 direct shadow and directional reconstruction fidelity
  - Retained 64x64 RGBA8 albedo cards; direct/total radiance cards are
    independently 128x128 in 512x512 HDR atlases, dispatched 16x16x8x8.
  - Screen probes now cover 8x8 pixels, with matching center reconstruction
    and updated probe allocations. The directional atlas stays 8x8 per probe.
  - Shadow rays use a small slope-aware receiver offset and retrace toward
    the actual light sample from the biased ray origin.
  - Both lit and direct card lookups clamp bilinear taps within their face.
  - Expect higher lighting-card and screen-probe GPU costs; benchmark on M2.
